WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 176: Franklin D. Roosevelt: D-Day, the Grand Alliance, and Yalta (1942–1945)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.600
[Rebecca] Today, we witnessed the epic climax of Franklin D.

4
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.920
[Rebecca] Roosevelt's 12-year presidency.

5
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:23.760
[Rebecca] Facing the combined military might of Nazi Germany and Imperial Japan, Roosevelt directed a global war effort that transformed the world order, all while his own physical body was steadily failing.

6
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:34.320
[Rebecca] Here is George with the Grand Alliance, the invasion of Normandy, and the final sacrifice of America's wartime commander-in-chief.

7
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:42.240
[George] When America entered World War II, its armed forces ranked 19th in the world, behind Portugal and Romania.

8
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.200
[George] In a matter of months, Roosevelt achieved what Adolf Hitler believed impossible.

9
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.080
[George] He converted the world's largest consumer economy into the ultimate machine of war.

10
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:59.280
[George] Automobile assembly lines stopped building sedans and turned out be 24 liberator bombers every 63 minutes.

11
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.280
[George] Shipyard workers like Rossi the Riveter built 10,000 ton Liberty ships in just four days.

12
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:18.480
[George] In January 1943, Roosevelt flew secretly across the submarine-infested Atlantic to Casablanca, Morocco, becoming the first sitting president to travel by air and the first to visit Africa.

13
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:26.640
[George] Meeting with Winston Churchill in a sunrenched villa, Roosevelt made a dramatic, historic announcement at their closing press conference.

14
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.960
[George] The Allies would accept nothing less than the unconditional surrender of Germany, Japan, and Italy.

15
00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:42.640
[George] Roosevelt was determined that the mistakes of 1918, when Germany was left to nurse grievance and claim it had been stabbed in the back, would never be repeated.

16
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.960
[George] Total destruction of militarism was the only path to enduring peace.

17
00:01:46.960 --> 00:02:00.760
[George] Roosevelt understood that modern mechanized warfare was fundamentally a contest of industrial logistics, raw steel production, and petroleum refinement, areas where American capacity was completely unrivaled on the globe.

18
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:12.600
[George] The industrial transformation of the American home front proved that a free society could outproduce totalitarian regimes through voluntary civic enterprise and technological superiority.

19
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:25.640
[George] In November 1943, Roosevelt undertook a hazardous journey to Tehran, Iran, for the historic first meeting of the Big Three: Roosevelt, Churchill, and Soviet Premier Joseph Stalin.

20
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:37.080
[George] Operating from a wheelchair in the Soviet embassy compound, Roosevelt acted as the crucial diplomatic bridge between the suspicious Soviet dictator and the romantic British prime minister.

21
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:50.520
[George] Stalin, whose Red Army had absorbed the unimaginable fury of the German Wehrmacht at Stalingrad and Kursk at the cost of millions of lives, bluntly demanded a firm commitment for the cross-channel invasion of Western Europe.

22
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:57.160
[George] Roosevelt gave his sacred word, Operation Overlord would be launched in May 1944.

23
00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:09.240
[George] When Churchill argued for alternative Mediterranean strikes through the Balkans, Roosevelt stood firm with Stalin, insisting that the decisive blow must fall directly on the Atlantic Wall of France.

24
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:16.920
[George] To command this colossal multinational invasion force, Roosevelt bypassed his brilliant chief of staff, George C.

25
00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:23.160
[George] Marshall, selecting instead a calm, tactful diplomat general from Kansas, General Dwight D.

26
00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.360
[George] Eisenhower.

27
00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:36.040
[George] By maintaining personal rapport with Stalin, Roosevelt preserved the crucial Eastern Front that absorbed two-thirds of the German army, preventing Hitler from concentrating his forces in the West.

28
00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:47.200
[George] Churchill and Stalin both recognized that Roosevelt was the indispensable linchpin holding the fragile Grand Alliance together against internal distrust and conflicting post-war ambitions.

29
00:03:47.200 --> 00:04:09.280
[George] In the pre-dawn darkness of June 6, 1944, the largest amphibious invasion fleet ever assembled, more than 5,000 ships, 11,000 aircraft, and 150,000 American, British, and Canadian soldiers hurled themselves against the concrete fortifications of Hitler's Atlantic Wall in Normandy.

30
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:17.680
[George] That night, as fierce battles raged on the bloody bluffs of Omaha and Utah beaches, President Roosevelt spoke to the nation over the radio.

31
00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:28.160
[George] But instead of delivering a boastful victory speech or a conventional military bulletin, Roosevelt asked the American people to join him in a solemn, heartfelt prayer.

32
00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:32.320
[George] Almighty God, his voice resonated through millions of homes.

33
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:44.000
[George] Our sons, pride of our nation, this day have set upon a mighty endeavor, a struggle to preserve our republic, our religion, and our civilization, and to set free a suffering humanity.

34
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:51.520
[George] Lead them straight and true, give strength to their arms, stoutness to their hearts, steadfastness in their faith.

35
00:04:51.520 --> 00:05:01.280
[George] Over 100 million Americans bowed their heads in churches, living rooms and city streets, united in spiritual communion as the liberation of Europe began.

36
00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:12.560
[George] That solemn radio prayer demonstrated Roosevelt's profound spiritual connection to the American people, framing the brutal crusade of liberation in terms of transcendent moral duty.

37
00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:23.840
[George] Across the United States, church bells rang and factories paused as citizens of all faiths joined their voices with their commander-in-chief in seeking divine protection for the invasion forces.

38
00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:29.120
[George] Behind the cheerful, confident facade, Franklin Roosevelt was a dying man.

39
00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:44.200
[George] In early 19th century, a comprehensive physical examination by Navy cardiologist Howard Brun revealed that the president was suffering from severe hypertension, advanced coronary arteriosclerosis, and congestive heart failure.

40
00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:50.600
[George] His blood pressure routinely spiked to terrifying levels of 220 over 120.

41
00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:56.440
[George] His heart was dangerously enlarged, and he suffered from chronic fatigue and shortness of breath.

42
00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:05.560
[George] His doctors warned him that continuing in office would be a death sentence, but Roosevelt was determined to finish the war and establish the United Nations.

43
00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:17.720
[George] Accepting an unprecedented nomination for a fourth term, Roosevelt replaced his controversial, left-wing vice president Henry Wallace with a dependable, plain-spoken Missouri senator, Harry S.

44
00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:18.600
[George] Truman.

45
00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:31.560
[George] To silence persistent rumors that he was on his deathbed, Roosevelt spent a torrential October day campaigning through the boroughs of New York in an open car, enduring freezing rain for four hours with his head held high.

46
00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:38.440
[George] In November 1944, the American electorate re elected him with 25 million votes over Thomas E.

47
00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:39.320
[George] Dewey.

48
00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:52.280
[George] His willingness to sacrifice his remaining physical vitality in the grueling campaign of 1944 was the ultimate testament to his unyielding sense of personal obligation to see the war through to final victory.

49
00:06:52.280 --> 00:07:03.000
[George] Roosevelt's determined campaign in the pouring rain of New York demonstrated his unshakable physical courage and his refusal to surrender to illness while the Republic was in peril.

50
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:16.320
[George] In February 1945, with Allied armies closing in on Germany from east and west, Roosevelt embarked on an exhausting 12,000 mile journey by warship and airplane to Yalta in the Soviet Crimea.

51
00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:25.040
[George] Meeting with Churchill and Stalin in the ornate, drafty Levadia Palace, Roosevelt looked gaunt, hollow-eyed, and shockingly frail.

52
00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.160
[George] Yet his strategic mind remained razor sharp.

53
00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:41.920
[George] His paramount objectives were twofold to secure Stalin's unreserved commitment to enter the Pacific War against Japan within three months of Germany's defeat, and to win Soviet participation in the new United Nations organization.

54
00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:49.360
[George] Stalin, holding millions of Red Army soldiers on the ground across Poland and Eastern Europe, drove a hard bargain.

55
00:07:49.360 --> 00:08:01.280
[George] Roosevelt insisted upon the declaration on liberated Europe, promising free and unfettered democratic elections in all liberated nations, a pledge Stalin would cynically betray within months.

56
00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:15.520
[George] Critics would later accuse Roosevelt of selling out Eastern Europe at Yalta, but as military historians recognized, Roosevelt could not sell what the Soviet army had already conquered with the blood of 27 million Soviet citizens.

57
00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:27.680
[George] Roosevelt recognized that without Soviet cooperation in the emerging United Nations, the postwar world would fracture into perpetual armed conflict, leading him to seek common ground wherever possible.

58
00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:38.080
[George] The Yalta agreements, though bitterly debated in later decades, reflected the harsh realities of military positions on the ground as the Red Army occupied Eastern Europe.

59
00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:45.200
[George] Returning from Yalta, Roosevelt delivered his final address to Congress on March 1, 1945.

60
00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:55.760
[George] For the first time in his public career, he apologized for remaining seated in his wheelchair, confessing that it was a relief not to have to carry ten pounds of steel around on his legs.

61
00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:07.960
[George] In late March, utterly exhausted, he retreated to his beloved Little White House in Warm Springs, Georgia, to rest before the opening of the founding United Nations Conference in San Francisco.

62
00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:20.520
[George] On the warm spring morning of April 12, 1945, Roosevelt sat in a leather armchair by the stone fireplace, signing official papers while artist Elizabeth Schumatoff painted his portrait.

63
00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:29.240
[George] At one o'clock in the afternoon, Roosevelt suddenly reached his left hand to his temple, whispering softly, I have a terrific headache.

64
00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:33.880
[George] His eyes closed, his head fell forward, and he slumped unconscious.

65
00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:37.160
[George] A massive cerebral hemorrhage had struck his brain.

66
00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:50.680
[George] At 3:35 that afternoon, Franklin Delano Roosevelt passed away at the age of 63, just weeks before Adolf Hitler committed suicide in his Berlin bunker and Nazi Germany surrendered unconditionally.

67
00:09:50.680 --> 00:10:01.720
[George] His sudden death on the threshold of triumph cast a profound shadow across the world, depriving humanity of its most visionary architect of post-war democratic reconciliation.

68
00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:12.360
[George] Warm Springs had been the birthplace of Roosevelt's physical rebirth after polio, making it fitting that he drew his final breath in the tranquil sanctuary where his courage had been forged.

69
00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:16.680
[George] News of Roosevelt's death shook the globe like a physical thunderbolt.

70
00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.440
[George] In London, Winston Churchill wept openly at his desk.

71
00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:29.640
[George] Across wartorn Europe and Pacific Foxholes, American soldiers and liberated concentration camp survivors broke down in uncontrollable grief.

72
00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:37.840
[George] As the presidential funeral train slowly carried Roosevelt's flag-draped coffin from Warm Springs to Washington and on to Hyde Park.

73
00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:50.000
[George] Hundreds of thousands of mourners lined the railroad tracks day and night, farmers in overalls holding hats to their chests, weeping women holding infants, and elderly veterans standing at rigid salute.

74
00:10:50.400 --> 00:11:04.320
[George] In Washington, an accordionist named Graham Jackson was photographed playing Going Home with tears pouring down his cheeks, capturing the heartbreak of a nation that had lost not merely a leader, but a protective father, Franklin D.

75
00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:14.720
[George] Roosevelt had guided America through the worst economic catastrophe in its history and led the democratic world to triumph over the darkest tyranny humanity had ever faced.

76
00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:23.040
[George] He left behind a modern superpower, the United Nations, and an eternal testament to the unconquerable spirit of human freedom.

77
00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:35.360
[George] Roosevelt left behind an America transformed into the undisputed leader of the free world, having saved democratic capitalism from depression and vanquished the most monstrous tyranny in recorded history.

78
00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:46.160
[George] His visionary leadership during twelve years of depression and world war established an enduring standard of presidential empathy, optimism, and global statesmanship.

79
00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:47.600
[Rebecca] Franklin D.

80
00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:55.680
[Rebecca] Roosevelt's 12-year presidency reshaped the 20th century and left an indelible imprint upon every corner of American life.

81
00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:07.840
[Rebecca] But with his sudden death, the monumental burden of finishing the global war and confronting the atomic age fell onto the shoulders of an unassuming former Haberdasher from Independence, Missouri, Harry S.

82
00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:08.960
[Rebecca] Truman.

83
00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:21.360
[Rebecca] Next week, we turned to the presidency of Harry Truman, the shocking secret of the Manhattan Project, the fateful decision to drop the atomic bomb on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, and the dawn of the Cold War.

84
00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:25.840
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

